course: history
lesson: 66
title: From band to nation: families, tribes, kingdoms, empires and countries
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Sam plays an empire game and paints the whole map his colour: «easy, just conquer everything». Amir guesses the globe has like 300 countries. Kofi thinks borders follow rivers and mountains. All three have it too simple.
explain: What really happened: real empires needed roads, messengers, governors, taxes and soldiers, many let local rulers stay, and most broke up when ruling got too hard. There are about 195 countries today, and many African borders were drawn by Europeans on maps, with little thought for the peoples living there.
explain: Think of how groups grow at school: a few friends, then a class, then the whole school with rules and a head teacher. Human groups grew the same way: small hunting bands of 20 to 50, then tribes and chiefdoms, then kingdoms once farming made extra food, then empires and countries.
explain: Leaders were not always takers. On the Pacific Northwest Coast of Canada, chiefs won respect by giving huge gifts at feasts called potlatches. Darius's grandad from Iran says Cyrus the Great let peoples keep their own gods, «bro»; that helped a huge empire stay calm.
explain: Many countries are young: Giulia's nonna, Italian for grandma, jokes that Italy is younger than her favourite cathedral. And people belong to many groups at once. Jamal from Birmingham says he is English, British, Black and a Villa fan, and yes, that is allowed.
fact: The scientist Robin Dunbar suggested a person can keep about 150 close relationships; hunter-gatherer bands were often 20 to 50 people, and the number 150 is still argued about.
fact: Among peoples of the Pacific Northwest, chiefs held potlatch feasts where they gave away food, blankets and goods; Canada banned the potlatch from 1885 to 1951.
fact: Farming made extra food that could feed soldiers, priests and builders; kings promised protection and collected part of the harvest, and Egypt became one kingdom around 3100 BCE.
fact: The Persian Empire around 500 BCE stretched from Egypt to India, and its kings often let peoples keep their religions and local leaders if they paid tribute, a regular payment of money or goods to the king.
fact: Real empires needed roads, governors, taxes and soldiers to hold together, and most broke up when ruling far-off lands got too hard.
fact: Italy became one country in 1861 and Germany in 1871; the idea that each people should have its own country spread in the 1800s.
fact: There are about 195 countries today; the United Nations has 193 members, but only 51 when it began in 1945.
fact: In 1884 and 1885 European powers met in Berlin to set rules for claiming Africa, and many borders were later drawn by Europeans on maps.
fact: In many parts of Europe surnames became common only between about 1300 and 1800; Iceland still names people after a parent, so Erik's son ends his name in -son and Erik's daughter ends hers in -dóttir, «daughter», while China has used family names much longer.
fact: In a federation, states or regions run some things and the national government runs others; the US, Switzerland, Germany, India and Australia are federations.
fact: People have always belonged to many groups at once: family, village, people, faith, country and the world.
exercise: How many close relationships did Robin Dunbar suggest a person can keep? | About 150 | About 15,000 | About 3 | About 1 million
exercise: How big were most hunter-gatherer bands? | About 20 to 50 people | About 5,000 people | About 1 million people | Just one person
exercise: What did chiefs do at a potlatch? | Gave away food, blankets and goods | Collected taxes from everyone there | Held a sports match for the young men | Wrote their laws down on a stone
exercise: When did Canada ban the potlatch? | From 1885 to 1951 | From 1066 to 1215 | From 2000 to 2020 | It was never banned
exercise: What made kings possible in early farming lands? | Extra food to feed soldiers, priests and builders | The invention of writing, which came before any farming | Too little food, so people needed someone to share it | The end of farming, when everyone went back to hunting
exercise: When did Egypt become one kingdom? | Around 3100 BCE | Around 1861 | Around 1945 | Around 300,000 years ago
exercise: How did Persian kings often treat conquered peoples? | Let them keep their religions if they paid tribute | Forced them all to give up their gods and pray to Persian ones | Gave them all free land and asked for nothing back | Sent them all far away to live on islands in the sea
exercise: What did empires need to hold together? | Roads, governors, taxes and soldiers | Only a flag and a royal song | Only one big map of their lands | Nothing; they held together on their own
exercise: When did Italy become one country? | In 1861 | In about 500 BCE | In 1066, with the Normans | In 1991
exercise: How many members does the United Nations have today? | 193 | 51 | 300 | 12
exercise: Why are some borders in Africa so straight? | Europeans drew many of them on maps | They follow straight rivers | Africans chose straight lines for fun | The Romans built walls there
exercise: What does «-dóttir» at the end of an Icelandic name mean? | Daughter of | Son of the father | King or queen of | From the town of
exercise: When did surnames become common in much of Europe? | Between about 1300 and 1800 | About 300,000 years ago | In the year 2000, with computers | Only in the last century or so
exercise: What is a federation? | A country where regions and the national government share power | A country with no national government at all, only towns and villages | A group of countries ruled together by one king or emperor | A country without borders, where anyone may come and go
exercise: Which of these is a federation? | Germany | Iceland, an island state | Egypt in 3100 BCE | The Library of Alexandria
exercise: Can a person belong to many groups at once? | Yes, family, city, country, faith and more | No, every person may belong to only one group | Only kings and queens can belong to more than one | Only at the weekend, never on school days
test: Ollie's mum says the famous guess is 150 mates. Is 150 a law of nature? | No, it is a guess that is still argued about | Yes, science proved it exactly in a lab | Yes, the 151st friend just vanishes from your life | No, the real number is exactly 5, it's in all the books
test: Liam thinks chiefs only ever took things from people. What did the potlatch show? | Many chiefs gained respect by giving | Chiefs never gave anything | Potlatches were tax offices | Chiefs were chosen by coins
test: Ethan asks why people wanted kings. What is the best answer? | Kings promised protection and organised the extra food | People wanted someone to do all their homework | Kings came down from the sky and everyone obeyed them | Nobody ever wanted kings; they just appeared
test: Darius's grandad says Cyrus let people keep their gods, bro. Why was that smart? | It helped a huge empire stay calm | It made the empire much smaller and weaker | It stopped all trade inside the empire | It meant nobody ever paid any taxes
test: Sam paints the map one colour in EU4 and says «just conquer everything». What would real rulers tell him? | Holding land was the hard part; rebellions and costs grew | Conquest was always easy and cheap, so go for it | Empires never broke up once the map was painted | Painting the whole map one colour always brought peace
test: Giulia's nonna says Italy is younger than her cathedral. How? | Italy became one country in 1861; the cathedral is older | Italy is 5,000 years old, so nonna is wrong | Cathedrals in Italy were all built last year | Nonna is just joking; Italy is older than any building
test: Amir guesses like 300 countries on the globe. Better guess? | About 195 | About 1,000, easy | About 12 or so | About 51, max
test: What would happen if a border were drawn straight through a people's land? | Families and peoples could be split apart | Everyone would happily move to one side | The land would slowly turn into a lake | Nothing, because borders never change anything for anyone
test: Björn's name means son of Erik. What would his sister Freya's surname end in? | -dóttir, meaning daughter | -son, exactly like Björn's | -ski, as in Polish names | She would have no surname at all
test: Emma asks if a federation is like a team of teams. Is she right? | Yes, regions run some things and the country runs others | No, a federation has no government | No, it is a type of football | Yes, and every team has its own army and money
test: Jamal: «English, British, Black and a Villa fan, is that allowed?» What is true? | Yes, most people belong to several groups at once | No, you must pick only one, like a football team | Only if you live in London, not in Birmingham | Only footballers are allowed to be several things
exam: Identify the approximate date at which Egypt was united as a single kingdom. | Circa three thousand one hundred BCE | Circa eighteen sixty-one of the common era | Circa nineteen forty-five | Circa five hundred CE
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the policy of the Persian kings towards subject peoples. | Subject peoples were frequently permitted to retain their religions upon payment of tribute | Subject peoples were obliged to abandon their religions and to worship the gods of Persia | Subject peoples were exempted from all obligations, including tribute | Subject peoples were relocated in their entirety to Europe
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises many present-day borders in Africa? | They were frequently drawn by European powers following the conference of 1884 to 1885 | They follow the ancient walls and roads constructed by the Roman Empire across the continent | They were chosen by referendum in every village | They have remained unaltered since the Stone Age
exam: Ascertain the number of member states of the United Nations at its foundation in 1945. | Fifty-one | One hundred and ninety-three | Three hundred | Twelve
